div.p:
Now, as it is thus plain how we are to speak in

 32. Arguments for the same taken conjointly.  reference to each [of the four simple cases], it will also be plain how we are to speak when taking them conjointly; supposing, for instance, either that yourself are willing to take the oath, but not to tender it; or, if you tender it, but are not willing to take it; or if you are inclined both to take it and to tender it;




or to do neither; for these cases must be made up of those above mentioned, so that our reasonings must also be made up of those above stated.
